

HTML → Understand webpage structure → Identify elements → Write Selenium locators → Automate
1. First understand HTML structure
A basic page look like
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>

<head>
    <title>Login Page</title>
</head>

<body>

    <h1>Login</h1>

    <input type="text" id="username">

    <input type="password" id="password">

    <button id="login">Login</button>

</body>

</html>

For Selenium, focus mainly on the elements inside <body>.

2. Learn HTML Tags.

A tag tells the browser what type of element it is.

Common tags you should know:

HTML Tag					Purpose								  Selenium Example	

<input>				Text box, password, etc.						Username field
<button>					Button									Login button
<a>		anchor			Link										Forgot Password
<select>					Dropdown									Country
<option>					Dropdown option							India
<textarea>				Large text box							Comments
<checkbox>				Usually <input type="checkbox">			Remember me
<radio>					Usually <input type="radio">				Gender
<div>					Container								Page sections
<span>					Inline container/text					Labels
<label>					Label for input							Username
<table>					Table									Employee data

3. Most Important: Attributes

For Selenium testing, attributes are extremely important.

<input type="text" id="username" name="userName">

Here;

input     → Tag
type      → Attribute
text      → Attribute value
id        → Attribute
username  → Attribute value
name      → Attribute
userName  → Attribute value

TAG + ATTRIBUTE + VALUE


HTML:
<input type="text" id="username">

selenium + java
driver.findElement(By.id("username"));

Complete example: java

WebElement username = driver.findElement(By.id("username"));

username.sendKeys("Jeetesh");

java
By.id("username")

Find the HTML element whose id is username.

5. Learn name HTML:
<input type="text" name="username">

Selenium + java
driver.findElement(By.name("username"));

Complete Code :

driver.findElement(By.name("username"))
      .sendKeys("Jeetesh");
      
