7. Learn Link Text HTML

<a href="forgot.html">Forgot Password?</a>

Selenium + Java
driver.findElement(By.linkText("Forgot Password?")).click();


8. Learn CSS Selectors (This is very important for Selenium.)

Suppose HTML is:

<input id="username" class="form-control" type="text">

We can locate it using:

ID : JAVA
By.cssSelector("#username")
Class: Java
By.cssSelector(".form-control")
Tag : Java
By.cssSelector("input")

Tag + ID JAVA
By.cssSelector("input#username")

Tag + Class JAVA
By.cssSelector("input.form-control")

9. Learn XPath
XPath is another extremely important Selenium skill.

HTML
<input id="username" name="user">

XPath JAVA
By.xpath("//input[@id='username']")

Example

By.xpath("//input[@name='user']")

Understand this structure
//input[@id='username']
  │      │       │
  │      │       └── Value
  │      └────────── Attribute
  └───────────────── Tag

10. Learn text() HTML
<button>Login</button>

XPATH
By.xpath("//button[text()='Login']")

This means:

Find the button whose visible text is Login.

11. Learn contains() HTML

<button id="loginButton123">Login</button>

We can use

By.xpath("//button[contains(@id,'loginButton')]")


12. Learn Parent-Child Relationships

This is very important when HTML becomes complicated (HTML)

<div id="login">

    <input id="username">

    <input id="password">

    <button>Login</button>

</div>

WE CAN UNDERSTAND THE STRUCTURE

div
 ├── input
 ├── input
 └── button
 
 XPath can use this relationship:
 //div[@id='login']//button
 Meaning:

Find the button inside the div whose id is login.

